            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that must lie on at least one of the three lines.\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Geometry of \\(\\mathcal P\\)\n\n* The three points \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) are collinear on  \n\n  \\[\n  D_{4}:\\;x+y=4\\qquad(\\text{slope }-1).\n  \\]\n\n* The two points \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) are collinear on  \n\n  \\[\n  D_{3}:\\;x+y=3\\qquad(\\text{slope }-1).\n  \\]\n\n* No other three points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) are collinear.  \n  Hence any line that is **not** horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\) (i.e. any sunny line) can contain at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\); if it contains two points, those points have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, and distinct sums \\(x+y\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Suppose a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists\n\nAssume three distinct lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) cover \\(\\mathcal P\\) and exactly two of them are sunny.\nWithout loss of generality let \\(L_{3}\\) be the **only** non‑sunny line.\nThus \\(L_{3}\\) is either\n\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n* vertical:   \\(x=c\\);\n* of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nDenote \\(\\mathcal P_{0}= \\mathcal P\\cap L_{3}\\) (points already covered by the non‑sunny line) and \\(\\mathcal P_{*}= \\mathcal P\\setminus\\mathcal P_{0}\\) (points that must be covered by the two sunny lines \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Analysis of the three possible types of \\(L_{3}\\)\n\n---\n\n##### (a)  \\(L_{3}\\) horizontal, \\(y=c\\)\n\nThe only horizontal lines meeting \\(\\mathcal P\\) are \\(y=1,2,3\\).\n\n*If \\(c=1\\):* \\(L_{3}\\) covers \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \nThen \\(\\mathcal P_{*}= \\{(1,2),(2,2),(1,3)\\}\\).  \nThe pair \\((1,2),(1,3)\\) shares the same \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(1\\); any line through them is vertical, hence non‑sunny.  \nA sunny line cannot contain both, so the two sunny lines cannot cover the three points of \\(\\mathcal P_{*}\\) – contradiction.\n\n*If \\(c=2\\):* \\(L_{3}\\) covers \\((1,2),(2,2)\\).  \nThen \\(\\mathcal P_{*}= \\{(1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(1,3)\\}\\).  \nThe pair \\((1,1),(1,3)\\) shares the same \\(x\\); again a sunny line cannot contain both, forcing at least three lines for \\(\\mathcal P_{*}\\) – contradiction.\n\n*If \\(c=3\\):* symmetric to the case \\(c=1\\).\n\nThus a horizontal non‑sunny line cannot occur.\n\n---\n\n##### (b)  \\(L_{3}\\) vertical, \\(x=c\\)\n\nThe only vertical lines meeting \\(\\mathcal P\\) are \\(x=1,2,3\\).\n\n*If \\(c=1\\):* \\(L_{3}\\) covers \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \nThen \\(\\mathcal P_{*}= \\{(2,1),(3,1),(2,2)\\}\\).  \nThe pair \\((2,1),(2,2)\\) shares the same \\(x\\); a sunny line cannot contain both – contradiction.\n\n*If \\(c=2\\):* \\(L_{3}\\) covers \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \nThen \\(\\mathcal P_{*}= \\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nThe pair \\((1,1),(1,2)\\) shares the same \\(x\\); again impossible.\n\n*If \\(c=3\\):* symmetric to the case \\(c=1\\).\n\nHence a vertical non‑sunny line is also impossible.\n\n---\n\n##### (c)  \\(L_{3}\\) of slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(x+y=c\\)\n\nThe only such lines containing points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) are \\(x+y=3\\) and \\(x+y=4\\).\n\n*If \\(c=3\\):* \\(L_{3}\\) covers \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  \nThen \\(\\mathcal P_{*}= \\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nThe pair \\((1,3),(3,1)\\) shares the same sum \\(4\\); any line through them is \\(x+y=4\\), which is non‑sunny.  \nThus they must lie on different sunny lines.  \nOne sunny line remains for the other two points; they can be placed together on the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)), but then we would need a third sunny line for either \\((1,3)\\) or \\((3,1)\\).  \nOnly two sunny lines are available – contradiction.\n\n*If \\(c=4\\):* \\(L_{3}\\) covers \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nThen \\(\\mathcal P_{*}= \\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nThe pair \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) shares the same sum \\(3\\); any line through them is \\(x+y=3\\), non‑sunny.  \nConsequently these two points cannot be on the same sunny line, so we would need three sunny lines to cover the three points of \\(\\mathcal P_{*}\\), contradicting the assumption of only two sunny lines.\n\nThus a non‑sunny line of slope \\(-1\\) also leads to a contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\nIn every possible choice for the unique non‑sunny line, the remaining points cannot be covered by only two sunny lines because they always contain a pair that forces a vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\) line. Therefore **no configuration of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines can satisfy the covering condition for \\(n=3\\).**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
