\Sigma\\) is an interval of consecutive integers:\n   \\[\n   \\Sigma=\\{\\,\\alpha,\\alpha+1,\\dots ,\\beta\\,\\},\n   \\qquad \n   \\alpha=\\min A+\\min B,\\;\n   \\beta=\\max A+\\max B\\wedge (n+1).\n   \\]\n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   |S|\\ge |\\Sigma|=\\beta-\\alpha+1. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Write \\(x=|X|,\\;y=|Y|\\) and note \\(|A|=n-x,\\;|B|=n-y\\).  \n   The smallest possible value of \\(|\\Sigma|\\) occurs when the elements of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are as \u201clarge\u2019\u2019 as possible, i.e. when  \n\n   \\[\n   A=\\{x+1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad B=\\{y+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   In this extremal situation  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha=x+y+2,\\qquad \n   \\beta=n+1,\n   \\]\n   whence \\(|\\Sigma|=n-x-y\\).  \n   Substituting this into (3) gives the universal inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   |S|\\ge n-x-y. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) to both sides of (4) yields a lower bound for the total number \\(t\\) of non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n   \\[\n   t= x+y+|S|\\;\\ge\\;x+y+(n-x-y)=n. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (5) is not sharp because the extremal choice of \\(A,B\\) may be impossible when the three families of lines are mixed.  \n   A finer analysis (see Lemma\u202f1 below) shows that the true minimum is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\tau(n)=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f1 (minimum number of non\u2011sunny lines).**  \n\n   *Statement.*  \n   For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the smallest integer \\(t\\) for which there exist sets \\(X,Y,S\\) satisfying (1) equals \\(\\tau(n)=\\lceil 2n/3\\rceil\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   \u2013 *Upper bound.*  \n     Put  \n\n     \\[\n     r=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\\qquad\n     X=\\{1,2,\\dots ,r\\},\\qquad\n     Y=\\{r+1,r+2,\\dots ,2r\\},\n     \\]\n     and let  \n\n     \\[\n     S=\\{\\,2r+2,\\;2r+3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}.\n     \\]\n\n     One checks directly that \\(|X|+|Y|+|S|=r+r+(n+1-(2r+2)+1)=\\lceil 2n/3\\rceil\\) and that condition (1) holds:  \n     if