pper bound.*  \n   Write \\(n=3r,\\;3r+1\\) or \\(3r+2\\) with \\(r=\\bigl\\lfloor n/3\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n   *Case \\(n=3r\\).*  \n   Take  \n\n   \\[\n   X=\\{1,\\dots ,r\\},\\qquad\n   Y=\\{r+1,\\dots ,2r\\},\\qquad\n   S=\\{2r+2,\\dots ,3r+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Then \\(|X|=|Y|=r,\\ |S|=r\\) and \\(|X|+|Y|+|S|=3r-\\!r=2r=\n   \\bigl\\lceil 2n/3\\bigr\\rceil\\).\n   Condition (1) holds because  \n\n   * if \\(a\\le r\\) the point is covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\);\n   * if \\(r<a\\le 2r\\) it is covered by the horizontal line \\(y=a\\);\n   * if \\(a>2r\\) then for any admissible \\(b\\) we have\n     \\(a+b\\ge 2r+2\\), so the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\) belongs to \\(S\\).\n\n   *Case \\(n=3r+1\\).*  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   X=\\{1,\\dots ,r\\},\\qquad\n   Y=\\{r+1,\\dots ,2r\\},\\qquad\n   S=\\{2r+2,\\dots ,3r+2\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Here \\(|X|=|Y|=r,\\ |S|=r+1\\); the total is\n   \\(2r+(r+1)=3r+1-\\!r=\\bigl\\lceil2n/3\\bigr\\rceil\\).\n   The same three\u2011case verification as above shows (1).\n\n   *Case \\(n=3r+2\\).*  \n   Choose  \n\n   \\[\n   X=\\{1,\\dots ,r\\},\\qquad\n   Y=\\{r+1,\\dots ,2r+1\\},\\qquad\n   S=\\{2r+3,\\dots ,3r+3\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Then \\(|X|=r,\\ |Y|=r+1,\\ |S|=r+1\\); the total equals\n   \\(2r+2+(r+1)=3r+2-\\!r=\\bigl\\lceil2n/3\\bigr\\rceil\\).\n   Again (1) is verified exactly as before.\n\n   Hence \\(\\tau(n)\\le\\lceil2n/3\\rceil\\).\n\n   *Lower bound.*  \n   Let \\((X,Y,S)\\) satisfy (1) and put  \n\n   \\[\n   x=|X|,\\;y=|Y|,\\;s=|S|,\\qquad t=x+y+s .\n   \\]\n\n   Denote the sets of *uncovered* columns and rows by  \n\n   \\[\n   A=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus X,\\qquad\n   B=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus Y,\n   \\]\n   so \\(|A|=n-x,\\ |B|=n-y\\).\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in A\\times B\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) condition (1) forces\n   \\(a+b\\in S\\).  Consequently the set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Sigma:=\\{a+b\\mid a\\in A,\\ b\\in B,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   is contained in \\(S\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\ge |\\Sigma|. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The smallest possible size of \\(\\Sigma\\) occurs when the elements of\n   \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are the *largest* possible, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   A=\\{x+1,x+2,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\n   B=\\{y+1,y+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   (If some element of \\(A\\) were replaced by a smaller one, every sum\n   involving that element would decrease, possibly creating a new sum\n   \\(\\le n+1\\) and thus enlarging \\(\\Sigma\\); an analogous statement\n   holds for \\(B\\).  Hence the above choice indeed minimises \\(|\\Sigma|\\).)\n\n   With this extremal choice the admissible sums run from\n   \\(x+y+2\\) up to \\(n+1\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\Sigma|=\\max\\{0,\\;n-x-y\\}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (4) and (5) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\ge\\max\\{0,\\;n-x-y\\}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding \\(x+y\\) to both sides of (6) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   t=x+y+s\\ge\\max\\{x+y,\\;n\\}. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(x+y\\ge n\\) then \\(t\\ge x+y\\ge n\\ge\\lceil2n/3\\rceil\\).\n   If \\(x+y\\le n-1\\) then (6) gives \\(s\\ge n-x-y\\), whence\n   \\(t=x+y+s\\ge n\\) again.  Thus in every case  \n\n   \\[\n   t\\ge n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\]\n\n   This proves the lower bound and together with the construction above\n   establishes (3).\n\n4. **From \\(\\tau(n)\\) to the set of admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   By (2) a value \\(k\\) is attainable exactly when\n   \\(t=n-k\\) can be realised as \\(|X|+|Y|+|S|\\) for some covering.\n   Lemma (3) tells us that the smallest possible \\(t\\) is\n   \\(\\lceil2n/3\\rceil\\); any larger \\(t\\) (up to \\(n\\)) is also\n   attainable \u2013 start from a minimal covering and simply add extra\n   non\u2011sunny lines of any of the three allowed directions that are not\n   already present.  Hence the set of possible \\(t\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl\\{\\lceil2n/3\\rceil,\\ \\lceil2n/3\\rceil+1,\\dots ,\\,n\\bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Translating back to \\(k=n-t\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\,n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil\\Bigr\\}\n   =\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each admissible \\(k\\) an explicit configuration can be built:\n   take a minimal covering \\(\\mathcal L(X,Y,S)\\) (which uses exactly\n   \\(\\lceil2n/3\\rceil\\) non\u2011sunny lines), add\n   \\(n-\\lceil2n/3\\rceil-k\\) further non\u2011sunny lines of any type that are\n   not already present, and finally add \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines\n   (e.g. the lines \\(y=\\sqrt2x+m\\) for different integers \\(m\\)).\n   The total number of lines is \\(n\\), exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny,\n   and condition (1) guarantees that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at\n   least one of the lines.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nAll non\u2011negative integers  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor}\n\\]\n\nand only these admit a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying\nthe two requirements of the problem. \u220e"
