 any \\((a,b)\\in A\\times B\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) condition (1) forces\n   \\(a+b\\in S\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   S\\supseteq\\Sigma:=\\{a+b\\mid a\\in A,\\ b\\in B,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad s\\ge|\\Sigma|. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   **Lemma 1.**  \n   For fixed sizes \\(|A|=n-x\\) and \\(|B|=n-y\\) the set \\(\\Sigma\\) is\n   smallest when  \n\n   \\[\n   A=\\{x+1,x+2,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\n   B=\\{y+1,y+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  Replacing an element of \\(A\\) (or \\(B\\)) by a larger one can only\n   increase every sum \\(a+b\\); therefore the number of sums that stay\n   \\(\\le n+1\\) cannot increase.  Repeating this operation yields the\n   claimed extremal choice. \u220e\n\n   With the extremal choice the admissible sums run from\n   \\(x+y+2\\) up to \\(n+1\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\Sigma|=\\max\\{0,\\;n-x-y\\}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (3) and (4) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\ge\\max\\{0,\\;n-x-y\\}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding \\(x+y\\) to both sides of (5) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   t=x+y+s\\ge\\max\\{x+y,\\;n\\}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(x+y\\le n\\) (otherwise the right\u2011hand side of (1) would already\n   hold for every point), (6) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   t\\ge n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus any family \\(\\mathcal L(X,Y,S)\\) satisfying (1) contains at least\n   \\(\\lceil2n/3\\rceil\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n\n   Write \\(n=3r+\\varepsilon\\) with \\(\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\) and put  \n\n   \\[\n   X:=\\{1,2,\\dots ,r\\},\\qquad\n   Y:=\\{r+1,r+2,\\dots ,2r\\},\\qquad\n   S:=\\{2r+2,2r+3,\\dots ,3r+\\varepsilon+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Cardinality.*  \n   \\(|X|=r,\\ |Y|=r,\\ |S|=r+\\varepsilon\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   t=|X|+|Y|+|S|=3r+\\varepsilon-\\!r\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification of (1).*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\