r any \\((a,b)\\in A\\times B\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) condition (1) forces\n   \\(a+b\\in S\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   S\\supseteq\\Sigma:=\\{a+b\\mid a\\in A,\\ b\\in B,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad s\\ge|\\Sigma|. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   **Lemma 1 (extremal choice).**  \n   *For fixed sizes \\(|A|=n-x\\) and \\(|B|=n-y\\) the set \\(\\Sigma\\) is\n   smallest when*  \n\n   \\[\n   A=\\{x+1,x+2,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\n   B=\\{y+1,y+2,\\dots ,n\\}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  Replace an element of \\(A\\) by a larger one (keeping the\n   cardinality unchanged).  All sums \\(a+b\\) with \\(b\\in B\\) increase,\n   therefore no new sum can become \\(\\le n+1\\); the number of admissible\n   sums cannot increase.  Repeating this operation until no element of\n   \\(A\\) can be increased yields the set displayed in (4).  The same\n   argument applies to \\(B\\). \u220e\n\n   With the extremal choice (4) the admissible sums run from\n   \\(x+y+2\\) up to \\(n+1\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\Sigma|=\\max\\{0,\\;n-x-y\\}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (3) and (5) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\ge\\max\\{0,\\;n-x-y\\}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding \\(x+y\\) to both sides of (6) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   t=x+y+s\\ge\\max\\{x+y,\\;n\\}. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The function \\(f(x,y)=\\max\\{x+y,\\;n\\}\\) is minimised, under the\n   constraints \\(0\\le x,y\\le n\\), when \\(x+y\\) is as small as possible\n   but still large enough to make the second argument dominate.  A\n   straightforward optimisation (consider the three cases\n   \\(x+y\\le n/3,\\; n/3\\le x+y\\le 2n/3,\\; x+y\\ge 2n/3\\)) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   t\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus any family \\(\\mathcal L(X,Y,S)\\) satisfying (1) contains at least\n   \\(\\lceil2n/3\\rceil\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n\n   Write \\(n=3r+\\varepsilon\\) with \\(\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\).\n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   X:=\\{1,2,\\dots 