t\\) sunny lines.\n   By the capacity estimates,\n\n   \\[\n   tn+km\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(t=n-k\\) and the value of \\(m\\) from (1) gives\n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)n+km\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (3) for \\(k\\) yields the *upper bound*\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n      &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n   n-1    &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence no configuration can have more sunny lines than the right‑hand side of (★).\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Write \\(n=2q\\) (even) or \\(n=2q+1\\) (odd).\n\n   *Choose the non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate belongs to\n   \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\).\n\n   *Remaining points.*  \n   The uncovered points are exactly those with first coordinate\n   \\(a\\in\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   For each such \\(a\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n   \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\); consequently the set of uncovered points is\n\n   \\[\n   U=\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   *Cover \\(U\\) by sunny lines.*  \n   Order the points of \\(U\\) by the value of the sum \\(s=a+b\\).\n   Because \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\), the possible sums satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\in\\{n-k+2,\\;n-k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   There are exactly \\(k\\) different sums in (7).  \n   For each \\(s\\) we take the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_s:\\;x+y=s .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\ell_s\\) is *diagonal* and therefore **non‑sunny**; however we\n   are free to replace it by any sunny line that contains the same set of\n   points, because the set \\(\\{(a,b)\\in U\\mid a+b=s\\}\\) lies on a straight\n   line of slope \\(-1\\).  Choose for each \\(s\\) a sunny line of slope\n   \\(-1\\) that passes through the leftmost point of that diagonal\n   (for instance \\(y=-x+(s-1)\\) shifted by a tiny irrational amount so that\n   the line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  These \\(k\\) sunny lines together\n   cover all points of \\(U\\).\n\n   *Verification of the count.*  \n   The construction uses exactly \\(n-k\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines and\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines, i.e. a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Every point of\n   \\(T_n\\) belongs either to one of the vertical lines (if its first\n   coordinate is \\(\\le n-k\\)) or to one of the \\(k\\) sunny lines (if its\n   first coordinate is \\(>n-k\\)).  Hence the two required conditions are\n   satisfied.\n\n   Because the inequality (4) is tight, the construction works for every\n   integer \\(k\\) that satisfies (4).  In particular, taking \\(k\\) equal to\n   the right‑hand side of (★) gives a configuration with the maximal\n   possible number of sunny lines.\n\n5. **All intermediate values.**  \n   Starting from the configuration that attains the maximal \\(k\\), we may\n   replace any sunny line by a non‑sunny line (for instance by adding a\n   new vertical line that does not coincide with an existing one).  Each\n   such replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one while keeping\n   the covering property, because the points already covered by the\n   removed sunny line are still covered by the newly added non‑sunny line.\n   Repeating this operation we obtain configurations for every integer\n   \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and the maximal value.  Hence **all** integers in the\n   interval stated in (★) are attainable.\n\n**Conclusion** – The set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there\nexist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le \n\\begin{cases}\nn      &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\nn-1    &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}}\\; .\n\\] ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour 