Because the construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) satisfying the bound of step\u202f3, all such \\(k\\) are attainable.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary bound derived from the capacity argument coincides with the constructive upper bound.  Therefore the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the set described in (\u2605).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le \n\\begin{cases}\nn      &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\nn-1    &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}}\\qquad\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **non\u2011sunny** iff it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Points on a sunny line  \n\nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains at least two lattice points.  \nLet \\((p,q)\\) be the primitive integer direction vector of \\(\\ell\\); thus\n\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), \\(p\\neq0\\), \\(q\\neq0\\) and \\(p+q\\neq0\\) (the last condition\nexcludes the diagonal \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \nIf \\((a_0,b_0)\\in\\ell\\cap\\mathbb Z^2\\), then all lattice points of \\(\\ell\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(a_i,b_i)=(a_0+ip,\\;b_0+iq),\\qquad i\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe sum \\(s_i:=a_i+b_i\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\ns_{i+1}-s_i=p+q\\neq0 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nHence the values \\(s_i\\) are distinct integers.  \nInside the triangle \\(T_n\\) we have \\(2\\le s_i\\le n+1\\); therefore the number of\nindices \\(i\\) for which \\((a_i,b_i)\\in T_n\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)-2}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBoth coordinates must stay positive.  From (1),\n\n\\[\na_i\\ge1\\Longrightarrow i\\ge -\\frac{a_0-1}{p},\n\\qquad\nb_i\\ge1\\Longrightarrow i\\ge -\\frac{b_0-1}{q}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|p|,|q|\\ge1\\), the most restrictive bound among (3) and the positivity\nconditions is obtained when \\(|p+q|=1\\).  Consequently a sunny line can contain at\nmost  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).  The bound is sharp: the line \\(y=x\\) (when \\(n\\) is odd) or\n\\(y=x-1\\) (when \\(n\\) is even) contains exactly \\(m\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A necessary inequality for the numbers of lines  \n\nSuppose we use \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\nA non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the vertical line\n\\(x=1\\) or the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) shows that the bound \\(n\\) is attainable).\nHence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)\\,n\\;+\\;k\\,m .\n\\]\n\nSince all \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) points must be covered, we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)n+k\\,m\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(m\\) from (4) and solve for \\(k\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2q+1\\) (odd):*  \\(m=q+1\\).  Equation (5) becomes  \n\n\\[\n(2q+1-k)(2q+1)+k(q+1)\\ge(2q+1)(q+1),\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to \\(k\\le2q+1=n\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2q\\) (even):*  \\(m=q\\).  Equation (5) becomes  \n\n\\[\n(2q-k)(2q)+kq\\ge q(2q+1),\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to \\(k\\le2q-1=n-1\\).\n\nThus any feasible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn      &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\\nn-1    &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nr:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non\u2011sunny lines}).\n\\]\n\n**(i) Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=r .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThese are distinct, non\u2011sunny, and they cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first\ncoordinate satisfies \\(a\\le r\\).\n\n**(ii) Points still uncovered.**  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nU:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid r+1\\le a\\le n\\}\n   =\\bigl\\{(r+s,\\;b)\\mid 1\\le s\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k+1-s\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(U\\) is a right\u2011angled triangular array containing exactly\n\\(\\displaystyle\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) lattice points.\n\n**(iii) Sunny lines covering \\(U\\).**  \nFor each \\(s=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_s:\\;y = x - (r+s) + (k+1-s)\n      \\;=\\;x - r -2s +k+1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_s\\) is \\(1\\); therefore \\(L_s\\) is sunny (it is not parallel\nto the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nA direct substitution shows that the integer points of \\(U\\) lying on \\(L_s\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{(r+s+i-1,\\;i)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,k+1-s\\bigr\\},\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\ni.e. the whole \\(s\\)-th column of the triangle.  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n|L_s\\cap U| = k+1-s .\n\\]\n\nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct because their intercepts\n\\(-r-2s+k+1\\) are different.  Moreover,\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{s=1}^{k} (L_s\\cap U)=U .\n\\]\n\n**(iv) Verification.**  \nThe total number of lines used is \\(r+(k)=n\\); all are distinct.  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) belongs either to a vertical line from (7) (if its first\ncoordinate \\(\\le r\\)) or to one of the sunny lines \\(L_s\\) (if its first\ncoordinate \\(>r\\)).  Hence both conditions of the problem are satisfied.\n\n**(v) Extremal values.**  \n\n* \\(k=0\\): take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n* \\(n\\) odd, \\(k=n\\): then \\(r=0\\) and the construction reduces to the family\n  \\(\\{L_s\\}_{s=1}^{n}\\), which indeed covers all of \\(T_n\\).  \n* \\(n\\) even, \\(k=n-1\\): then \\(r=1\\); we use the single vertical line \\(x=1\\)\n  together with the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(L_s\\) (\\(s=1,\\dots ,n-1\\)).\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) satisfying (6) we have exhibited a concrete\nconfiguration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final answer  \n\nCombining the necessary bound (6) with the explicit construction, we obtain that\nthe admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely those described in (\u2605).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le \n\\begin{cases}\nn      &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\nn-1    &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}}\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]"
