\n> \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), \\(p\\neq0\\), \\(q\\neq0\\) and \\(p+q\\neq0\\) (the last condition\n> excludes the diagonal \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  The conditions correctly characterise a sunny line (not horizontal, not vertical, not slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n#### 2.2  Lattice points on the line\n\n> \u201cIf \\((a_0,b_0)\\in\\ell\\cap\\mathbb Z^2\\), then all lattice points of \\(\\ell\\) are  \n> \\[\n> (a_i,b_i)=(a_0+ip,\\;b_0+iq),\\qquad i\\in\\mathbb Z .\n> \\tag{1}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  This is the standard parametrisation of integer points on a line with primitive direction vector.\n\n#### 2.3  Behaviour of the sum \\(s_i=a_i+b_i\\)\n\n> \u201cThe sum \\(s_i:=a_i+b_i\\) satisfies  \n> \\[\n> s_{i+1}-s_i=p+q\\neq0 .\n> \\tag{2}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  Immediate from the definition of \\((a_i,b_i)\\).\n\n#### 2.4  Upper bound from the sum constraint\n\n> \u201cHence the values \\(s_i\\) are distinct integers.  \n> Inside the triangle \\(T_n\\) we have \\(2\\le s_i\\le n+1\\); therefore the number of\n> indices \\(i\\) for which \\((a_i,b_i)\\in T_n\\) is at most  \n> \\[\n> \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)-2}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n>   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n> \\tag{3}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  The range of possible sums has length \\(n-1\\); spacing by \\(|p+q|\\) yields the stated bound.\n\n#### 2.5  Positivity constraints and the claim about \\(|p+q|=1\\)\n\n> \u201cBoth coordinates must stay positive.  From (1),  \n> \\[\n> a_i\\ge1\\Longrightarrow i\\ge -\\frac{a_0-1}{p},\n> \\qquad\n> b_i\\ge1\\Longrightarrow i\\ge -\\frac{b_0-1}{q}.\n> \\]\n> Since \\(|p|,|q|\\ge1\\), the most restrictive bound among (3) and the positivity\n> conditions is obtained when \\(|p+q|=1\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap.**  \n*Why it is a gap:*  \n- For a sunny line we have \\(p\\neq0\\) and \\(q\\neq0\\); consequently \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\).  \n- The statement \u201cthe most restrictive bound \u2026 is obtained when \\(|p+