ch simplifies to \\(k\\le2q+1=n\\).  \n> *If \\(n=2q\\) (even):*  \\(m=q\\).  Equation (5) becomes \u2026 which simplifies to \\(k\\le2q-1=n-1\\).\u201d\n\n*Correct algebra.*  The derivation yields the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn      &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\\nn-1    &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus the **necessary** part of the answer is sound.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  \u201cConstruction for every admissible \\(k\\)\u201d\n\n#### 4.1  Definition of \\(r\\) and the non\u2011sunny lines\n\n> \u201cLet \\(r:=n-k\\) (the number of non\u2011sunny lines).  \n> **(i) Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n> Take the vertical lines  \n> \\[\n> x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=r .\n> \\tag{7}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Correct* for the non\u2011sunny part; these lines are distinct and cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le r\\).\n\n#### 4.2  Description of the uncovered set \\(U\\)\n\n> \u201c**(ii) Points still uncovered.**  \n> Define  \n> \\[\n> U:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid r+1\\le a\\le n\\}\n>   =\\bigl\\{(r+s,\\;b)\\mid 1\\le s\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k+1-s\\bigr\\}.\n> \\tag{8}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  The set \\(U\\) is precisely the right\u2011angled triangle of size \\(k\\); its cardinality \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) is correctly computed.\n\n#### 4.3  The proposed sunny lines\n\n> \u201c**(iii) Sunny lines covering \\(U\\).**  \n> For each \\(s=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n> \\[\n> L_s:\\;y = x - (r+s) + (k+1-s)\n>      \\;=\\;x - r -2s +k+1 .\n> \\tag{9}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Correct* that each \\(L_s\\) has slope\u202f1, hence is sunny, and that the intercepts are distinct.\n\n#### 4.4  Claim that each \\(L_s\\) contains an entire column of \\(U\\)\n\n> \u201cA direct substitution shows that the integer points of \\(U\\) lying on \\(L_s\\) are  \n> \\[\n> \\bigl\\{(r+s+i-1,\\;i)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,k+1-s\\bigr\\},\n> \\tag{10}\n> \\]  \n> i.e. the whole \\(s\\)-th column of the triangle. Consequently \\(|L_s\\cap U| = k+1-s\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error.**  \n*Why it is false:*  \nTake a generic point of the claimed set, \\((a,b)=(r+s+i-1,i)\\). Substituting into the equation of \\(L_s\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ni \\stackrel{?}{=} (r+s+i-1) - r - 2s + k + 1\n   = i - s + k .\n\\]\n\nThis equality forces \\(s=k\\); for any \\(s\\neq k\\) the point does **not** satisfy the line equation. Hence the set (10) is **not** contained in \\(L_s\\). In fact each line \\(L_s\\) meets the column \\(a=r+s\\) only at its top point \\((r+s,\\,k+1-s)\\); it does **not** contain the lower points of that column.\n\n*Consequences:*  The claim that each \\(L_s\\) covers an entire column is false, so the union \\(\\bigcup_{s=1}^{k}(L_s\\cap U)\\) is far smaller than \\(U\\). The construction therefore fails to cover all points of \\(U\\).\n\n#### 4.5  Claim of complete coverage\n\n> \u201cThe \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct because their intercepts\n> \\(-r-2s+k+1\\) are different.  Moreover,\n> \\[\n> \\bigcup_{s=1}^{k} (L_s\\cap U)=U .\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error.**  This statement directly follows from the false claim (10).  As shown above, many points of \\(U\\) (for example \\((r+s,1)\\) when \\(s<k\\)) are not on any \\(L_s\\).  Hence the union is **not** equal to \\(U\\).\n\n#### 4.6  Verification of the whole configuration\n\n> \u201cThe total number of lines used is \\(r+(k)=n\\); all are distinct.  \n> Every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs either to a vertical line from (7) (if its first\n> coordinate \\(\\le r\\)) or to one of the sunny lines \\(L_s\\) (if its first\n> coordinate \\(>r\\)).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error.**  Because the sunny lines do not cover all points with first coordinate \\(>r\\), the configuration does **not** satisfy the problem\u2019s covering requirement.\n\n#### 4.7  Special cases\n\n> \u201c* \\(k=0\\): take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n> * \\(n\\) odd, \\(k=n\\): then \\(r=0\\) and the construction reduces to the family\n>   \\(\\{L_s\\}_{s=1}^{n}\\), which indeed covers all of \\(T_n\\).  \n> * \\(n\\) even, \\(k=n-1\\): then \\(r=1\\); we use the single vertical line\n>   \\(x=1\\) together with the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(L_s\\) (\\(s=1,\\dots ,n-1\\)).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error.**  The claim that the family \\(\\{L_s\\}_{s=1}^{n}\\) (for odd \\(n\\)) or \\(\\{L_s\\}_{s=1}^{n-1}\\) (for even \\(n\\)) covers all of \\(T_n\\) is false, as demonstrated by explicit counter\u2011examples (e.g. for \\(n=5\\) the point \\((1,2)\\) is uncovered).  Hence these special\u2011case constructions are also invalid.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe **necessary** condition (6) derived in Sections\u202f1\u20132 is correct.  However, the **sufficiency** part (Section\u202f3) is fundamentally flawed: the proposed sunny lines do not cover the required set of points, and the argument contains multiple critical errors.  Consequently the solution does **not** establish the existence of configurations for the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n**Overall assessment:** The solution is invalid due to critical errors in the construction and a justification gap in the bound for a sunny line."

