1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because of (1) we have \\(|p|\\ge1,\\;|q|\\ge1\\) and \\(|p+q|\\ge1\\).  \n   If \\(|p+q|=1\\) then, from (1), one of \\(|p|,|q|\\) is at least\u202f2; in that case the bound (4) is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{1}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1=n .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   When \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\) the bound (4) is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Comparing (5) and (6) we see that the *global* maximum number of points of \\(T_n\\) that a sunny line can contain is  \n\n   \\[\n   m:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The bound is sharp:  \n\n   * if \\(n\\) is odd, the line \\(y=x\\) contains the points \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots,\\bigl(\\frac{n+1}{2},\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr)\\), i.e. \\(m\\) points;  \n   * if \\(n\\) is even, the line \\(y=x-1\\) contains the points \\((2,1),(3,2),\\dots,\\bigl(\\frac{n}{2}+1,\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr)\\), again \\(m\\) points.\n\n3. **A necessary inequality for the numbers of sunny and non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n\n   Suppose we use \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   A non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the vertical line \\(x=1\\) or the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) shows that the bound \\(n\\) is attainable).  \n   Therefore the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)\\,n\\;+\\;k\\,m .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Since all \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) points must be covered, we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)n+k\\,m\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(m\\) from (7) and solving (9) for \\(k\\) yields  \n\n   * if \\(n=2q+1\\) (odd) then \\(m=q+1\\) and (9) gives \\(k\\le2q+1=n\\);  \n   * if \\(n=2q\\) (even) then \\(m=q\\) and (9) gives \\(k\\le2q-1=n-1\\).\n\n   Hence any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n  